# Camera worker pitfalls

These were each found by debugging real hardware. Re-deriving them is expensive.

## Device caps are not available in READY

`mfvideosrc` opens the device on the READY→PAUSED transition, so a caps query in
READY returns template ranges (`width=[1,32768]`), not real modes. Enumerating
modes from that yields nothing. Read the device's native Media Foundation media
types instead (`capture_mode.cpp`), and only fall back to a GStreamer probe in
PAUSED.

## Windows hides MJPEG on many UVC devices

The Windows frame server transparently decodes MJPEG and advertises only raw
types. On an MX Brio, `image/jpeg` caps fail with `Internal data stream error`
even though the hardware produces MJPEG. Never prefer `image/jpeg` on bandwidth
grounds; prefer raw, and pick NV12 first so the record branch's `videoconvert`
becomes a passthrough.

## Send EOS before NULL

`splitmuxsink` and `matroskamux` only finalize the open fragment on EOS.
Dropping straight to `GST_STATE_NULL` leaves a 0-byte `.mkv` beside a fully
populated timing sidecar.

## Never paper over a slow device

A camera may deliver below the negotiated rate (auto-exposure lengthens
integration time in dim light). Record what arrived; do not duplicate frames or
retime to hit the nominal rate. The timing sidecar is the honest record.
